// What a test is.
//
// Today: `export string test_adds()`. The runner enumerates exports whose names begin with `test`,
// calls each with no arguments, and reads a string — empty for pass. Three consequences, all of
// them from the signature being fixed:
//
//   - **A test cannot ask for anything.** It takes no parameters, so a test that needs to read a
//     file cannot be written in wac at all and has to live host-side. `packages/wactest/src/host.wac`
//     and `built.wac` exist to work around exactly this.
//   - **A test cannot be async**, for the same reason: there is nowhere for a ticket to go.
//   - **The name is the contract.** `test` as a prefix is a string convention that nothing checks,
//     and `harness/testRegistrars.ts` exists to keep two lists of spellings in step — they went out
//     of step once and 28 tests went missing from MAP.
//
// None of that is about testing. It is about a module boundary that could only carry a string.

import { Report } from "./assert.wac";
import { Ticket } from "core";
import { Files, Clock, NotGranted } from "std";

/**
 * A test is a function that takes what it needs and answers a `Report`.
 *
 * These four are the shapes, and **the runner tells them apart by reading the export's type**, not
 * by its name. A wasm export carries its signature; `bindgen` already reads them to generate a
 * TypeScript surface, so the information is there and is checked by the engine rather than agreed
 * by convention.
 *
 *     export Report test_adds()                          // pure — no authority, cannot touch anything
 *     export Report test_parses(Fixtures f)              // wants data, still cannot touch the world
 *     export async Report test_reads(Files files)        // wants one capability, and is awaited
 *     export async Report test_serves(Net net, Tasks t)  // wants two, one of them a scheduler
 *
 * The first shape is the interesting one. A test that takes nothing **provably** cannot read a
 * file, open a port or see the clock — the same claim the language makes about any function,
 * applied to the thing that is supposed to be checking the others. Today every test has exactly as
 * much authority as the runner, because the runner is what wasm gives the module.
 *
 * **The last two said `Sys sys` until the bundle was deleted, and the change is not cosmetic.** With
 * a bundle a test asking for authority asks for *all* of it, so the signature says only *this one
 * touches the world* — three of the four shapes above collapse to two. Without one, the signature is
 * the grant: `test_reads(Files)` cannot open a port, and a reader learns that from the line rather
 * than from the body.
 *
 * It also makes the enumeration harder in a way worth stating. Matching *does this export take a
 * `Sys`* is one type test; matching *which of nine projections does it take, in what order* is a
 * signature to read. That is the same work `bindgen` already does and it is more of it, so the
 * runner's job grows as the grant narrows — which is the trade rather than a snag.
 */

/**
 * What a runner hands a test that asked for a capability.
 *
 * **A fake is an ordinary value**, which is the whole of it. A capability is not a singleton and
 * nothing recognises a ticket by its type, so a test's capability is one it built, its tickets settle
 * when the test says so, and its clock is whatever it was constructed with. None of that needs a seam,
 * an injection point, or a mode flag in the real one — the seam is the parameter.
 *
 * **And the projections are what make it small.** Against one flat capability of sixty-two methods,
 * faking two of them means declaring a subtype of the whole thing and leaving sixty inherited from
 * something that traps. Against groups, a fake is one small struct: `FakeFiles` overrides the two
 * methods `Files` has that the test uses, and a test that never touches the network never mentions
 * `Net`. That is an argument for the grouping that only appears here, in the package whose entire
 * job is standing in for the real thing.
 */
// **A fake is the same struct, not a subclass.** These were `struct FakeFiles : Files` with an
// `override`, which needs dynamic dispatch — and dispatch is static, so the override never ran. The
// projections are structs of funcrefs now, so a fake is a `Files` holding different funcrefs, and
// the thing that varies is a lambda rather than a type.
export Files fakeFiles(Map<string, u8[]> content) {
  return Files(
    (string p) => memRead(content, p),
    (string p, u8[] b) => memWrite(content, p, b),
    (string p) => Err(NotGranted(p)),
    (string p) => Err(NotGranted(p)),
    (string p, bool parents) => Err(NotGranted(p)),
    (string p, bool recursive) => Err(NotGranted(p)),
    (string from, string to) => Err(NotGranted(from)),
    (string p) => Err(NotGranted(p)),
  );
}

async Result<u8[], NotGranted> memRead(Map<string, u8[]> content, string path) {
  u8[]? got = content.get(path);
  return got is null ? Result.Err(NotGranted(path)) : Result.Ok(got!);
}

async Result<void, NotGranted> memWrite(Map<string, u8[]> content, string path, u8[] b) { … }

/** Fixed, because a test that reads the clock twice should get the same answer unless it says not. */
/** Fixed, because a test that reads the clock twice should get the same answer unless it says not. */
export Clock fakeClock(i64 at) {
  return Clock(() => at, () => at * 1000000);
}

/**
 * A test that asserts something is *refused* needs a canary, and the canary is a second grant.
 *
 * Proving a refusal means proving the capability could have worked. A test that hands over a `Files`
 * with nothing in it and checks for `NotGranted` passes whether the guard exists or not. So the shape is
 * two calls against two grants: one that must succeed, one that must not.
 */
export async Report checkRefused<V, E>(
  T t,
  fn<Ticket<Result<V, E>>()> allowed,
  fn<Ticket<Result<V, E>>()> refused,
  string what,
) {
  t.isTrue((await allowed()).isOk(), "\{what}: the canary grant should have worked");
  t.isTrue((await refused()).isErr(), "\{what}: the narrowed grant should have been refused");
  return t.report();
}
